Goal:
To bypass the .bashrc file that is on the level that automatically disconnects us because shell
for the next level is not /bin/bash.

Connect:
ssh -T bandit18@bandit.labs.overthewire.org -p2220

Approach:
So, according to this room the .bashrc file is configured in such a way that if we spawn an interactive
shell we are kicked out immediately.

I googled these exact words to figure this out "How to bypass the .bashrc file"

There are multiple ways to solve this room one obvious way was to remove the file itself by
"ssh -t user@host "rm -rf .bashrc"" -> yeilded permission denied instead of exit.


Command:
*{ssh -T user@host port}*
this is used to launch the next level without an interactive terminal.

*{ssh -t user@host port /bin/sh}*
(sh,dash) these are the two shells that work with experimenting by me.
The -t is used to specify a particular shell to launch as the user at host.

Breakdown:
both the options that were used in conjuction to are nearly identical one indicates that we don't
wish to spawn any terminal(-T) while the other specifies as to which terminal we want to use.

Result:
We are able to login as user at host without an interactive terminal and extract the password from
the home dir successfully

Key Takeaway:
This room showcased the importance of knowledge about sheels/terminals (both are not the same thing).
